You are debugging a production issue where a high-throughput microservice was recently upgraded to Java 21 Virtual Threads. Under load testing, thread dump logs indicate severe latency spikes and carrier thread starvation. Upon reviewing the codebase, you find the following snippet used for fetching cached API response tokens:

java
@Component
public class AuthTokenCache {
    private final Map<String, String> cache = new HashMap<>();

    public synchronized String getToken(String clientId) {
        if (!cache.containsKey(clientId)) {
            cache.put(clientId, fetchTokenFromRemoteAuthServer(clientId)); // Blocking Network I/O
        }
        return cache.get(clientId);
    }
}

You are debugging a production issue where an order fulfillment microservice leaves orphaned records in the database. When an order fails during async notification processing, the primary database changes executed in the outer method are not rolling back as expected.

java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    @Autowired private OrderRepository orderRepo;
    @Autowired private NotificationService notificationService;

    @Transactional
    public CompletableFuture<Void> processOrder(Order order) {
        orderRepo.save(order);
        return CompletableFuture.runAsync(() -> {
            notificationService.sendConfirmation(order);
            if (order.isHighPriority()) {
                throw new RuntimeException("Failed to send priority notification");
            }
        });
    }
}

You are debugging a production issue where a Spring Boot API returns generic 500 Internal Server Errors instead of specific 402 Payment Required status codes. The global exception handler (`@ControllerAdvice`) is designed to catch `PaymentFailedException`, but logs reveal that the exception handler is receiving an unhandled exception wrapper instead.

